steps.body:
A sorted array rotated at some point, like `[40, 50, 60, 10, 20, 30]`, is no longer sorted, so the plain halving can throw away the half that holds the target. But cut any range in the middle and at least one half is still in order. Find that half by comparing its two ends, check whether the target lies between them, and keep the half that can hold it:

```javascript
function searchRotated(arr, target) {
  let lo = 0;
  let hi = arr.length - 1;
  while (lo <= hi) {
    const mid = Math.floor((lo + hi) / 2);
    if (arr[mid] === target) {
      return mid;
    }
    if (arr[lo] <= arr[mid]) {
      // the left half is in order
      if (arr[lo] <= target && target < arr[mid]) {
        hi = mid - 1;
      } else {
        lo = mid + 1;
      }
    } else {
      // the right half is in order
      if (arr[mid] < target && target <= arr[hi]) {
        lo = mid + 1;
      } else {
        hi = mid - 1;
      }
    }
  }
  return -1;
}
console.log(searchRotated([40, 50, 60, 10, 20, 30], 20)); // 4
console.log(searchRotated([40, 50, 60, 10, 20, 30], 35)); // -1
```

`arr[lo] <= arr[mid]` means the left half climbs with no break in it. When the target isn't inside the half that is in order, it can only be in the other half, break and all. Each step still throws away half of what is left, so the search stays O(log n). It needs distinct values: in `[1, 3, 1, 1, 1]` both ends of a half can be equal around the break, and the search misses the 3.

**The gotcha:** keep the `<=` in `arr[lo] <= arr[mid]`. When `lo` and `mid` are the same index, that one-item half is in order. With `<`, a search for 1 in `[3, 1]` takes the wrong branch and returns `-1`.

